ically  high now all of a sudden we have  historically low my big takeaway from  this entire situation is this is what  happens when we have market intervention  we do not have a free market when it  comes to mortgages why was mortgage  demand so high last year it's because  the central bank intervened and they put  interest rates down to zero and they  started buying up assets in the market  that led to a boom in mortgage demand  and people went out and they spent a lot  of money and they pushed home prices  higher sometimes as much as 20 percent  higher on average but now all of a  sudden the federal reserve and other  central banks they're intervening in the  market again they're trying to bring  those interest rates higher and they now  want to be net sellers in the market any  time that you have market intervention  you get some sort of synthetic response  in the market and that's what we're  seeing with a 22-year low in mortgage  demand  ultimately the free market is smarter  than economist the free market is  smarter than central bankers and the  free market is smarter than politicians  they should get out of the way they  should stop intervening and they should  allow the free market to reign if we  went back to free markets we wouldn't  have all this volatility we wouldn't  have historic highs and historic lows  back to back years  ultimately we need the free market  because the free market knows best the  free market would knock off all this  nonsense mortgage demand is low my guess  is once the fed pivots we'll go right  back in the other direction it's like a  roller coaster go up we go down we go up  we go down fed's in control that's not  the way that an economy is supposed to  work that's no way for individuals or  businesses to try to plan their  financial balance sheets and their  investment schedule over the coming  years uncertainty is bad for economies  and the more market intervention we have  the more uncertainties we have and if we  have uncer